---
// Traduction anglaise de /integration-ia/ le lien "Notre approche
// intégration IA en entreprise" de la home EN pointe ici.
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../styles/legacy-article.css";

const title = "AI Integration in Business: From POC to Production";
const description =
  "AI integration in business: from POC to real production. Governance, operational workflows, independent teams on exit. For mid-market companies. Get in touch.";

const serviceSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  serviceType: "AI Integration in Business",
  name: title,
  description,
  url: "https://izybiz.fr/en/ai-integration/",
  areaServed: "France",
  provider: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "Izybiz",
    url: "https://izybiz.fr",
  },
};
---

<Layout lang="en" title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/en/ai-integration/">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <main class="legacy-article">
    <article class="legacy-article__article">
      <a class="legacy-article__back" href="/en/">Home</a>
      <h1 class="legacy-article__title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="legacy-article__lede">{description}</p>

      <div class="legacy-article__body">
        <p>
          95% of enterprise GenAI projects fail. 84% because of management not
          technology.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's not a tooling problem. It's a governance problem, a use-case
          framing problem, and a matter of holding execution through to
          production. That's exactly where Izybiz steps in.
        </p>
        <p class="legacy-article__cta-line">
          <a class="legacy-article__cta" href="/en/contact/" data-cta="mid-page">Discuss your situation</a>
        </p>


        <h2>The real problem with AI integration projects in business</h2>
        <p>
          23% of executives can connect their AI investments to a measurable
          financial outcome. The other 77% have POCs, demos, pilot projects and
          nothing genuinely running in production.
        </p>
        <p>The causes are well known:</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Use-case framing is wrong.</strong> Projects start from the
          tool's capabilities rather than the operational problems to solve. The
          result: technically successful projects that change nothing in
          operations.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Governance isn't in place.</strong> Who decides when an AI
          output is reliable? Who validates? Who handles exceptions? Without
          defined human checkpoints, teams don't adopt or adopt haphazardly.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>There's no operational lead.</strong> IT drives the technology.
          The business drives the results. The two don't talk. Nobody owns the
          integration as a whole.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Dependency on the vendor or integrator isn't anticipated.</strong>
          By the end of the project, the company doesn't understand what it
          deployed and can't evolve it alone.
        </p>


        <h2>AI integration in business: the Make or Buy trade-off</h2>
        <p>
          When an AI integration project starts, the CEO's first question is
          almost always the same: do we build it in-house, or bring someone in?
        </p>
        <p>
          Both options have their limits. Knowing them avoids making the wrong
          choice for the wrong reasons.
        </p>

        <h3>Make Delegate to IT</h3>
        <p>
          IT is structurally under pressure. That's not a criticism it's the
          result of ten years of IT cost optimisation. Headcount has been
          rationalised, budgets trimmed, teams sized to keep the lights on. The
          result: IT is chronically overloaded. That's not an anomaly, it's a
          permanent, accepted state.
        </p>
        <p>
          Adding an AI integration project without dedicated resources means
          choosing between two bad options: overload teams that are already
          stretched, or watch the project sit at low priority between two
          critical tickets. Either way, the project doesn't land.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the real question isn't "IT or not IT" that's a false dilemma. The
          right question is: <strong>how do you bring IT in without drowning it,
          and who holds the thread between technology and business
          operations?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Izybiz isn't here to replace IT. It's here to spare IT from carrying
          alone a project that exceeds its natural scope. Make or buy,
          architecture, vendor selection, build/integrate/delegate trade-offs
          these are decisions made with IT, not instead of it. Once the choices
          are made, IT takes back what's its territory: run, maintenance,
          security. That's its mission. It fulfils it.
        </p>

        <h3>Buy Trust an external provider</h3>
        <p>
          The alternative to Make is Buy. Hand the project to an external
          provider: an AI vendor, an IT services firm, a consulting firm. The
          problem is that all three have structural limits and often, interests
          that don't align with yours.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>AI vendors</strong> sell you their tool. Their business model
          relies on adoption and dependency on their platform not on your
          independence. Vendor selection should never be left to the vendor
          itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>IT services firms</strong> deploy technical teams developers,
          data engineers, integrators. They execute what they're asked to build.
          What they don't do: define what to build, frame business use cases,
          drive change within teams, or own business results. Result: they
          deliver a technically functional system your teams don't adopt and are
          often the only ones who understand. The keys to the truck they don't
          hand them over: their business model relies on continuity of service.
        </p>
        <p>
          On top of that, there's a rarely disclosed conflict of interest: most
          large IT services firms hold commercial partnerships with AI solution
          publishers Microsoft, Google, Salesforce, ServiceNow. These
          agreements steer recommendations, even unconsciously. Making you
          independent from a vendor isn't in their economic interest.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Consulting firms</strong> produce AI strategies. Diagnostics,
          roadmaps, architecture recommendations. What they don't do: hold
          execution, drive operational change, or answer for results in
          production. They tell you what to do. Not how to make it hold over
          time.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Izybiz is neither.</strong> Independent from any vendor, with no
          business model built on dependency, with a single objective: that by
          the end of the mission, your organisation runs on its own IT
          trained, workflows documented, teams autonomous. The keys to the
          truck you have them.
        </p>


        <h2>What AI integration in business produces when it works</h2>
        <p>When an AI integration is well executed, the client comes out with:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>An AI system in production</strong> not a POC, not a demo. A workflow running, with real volumes.</li>
          <li><strong>Documented workflows</strong> every step, every human checkpoint, every validation rule.</li>
          <li><strong>A trained team</strong> that understands what it uses, can evolve it, and isn't dependent on a consultant.</li>
          <li><strong>Clear responsibilities</strong> who does what, who validates what, how incidents are handled.</li>
          <li><strong>Total independence</strong> Izybiz leaves. The organisation carries on.</li>
        </ul>


        <h2>The Izybiz method for AI integration in business</h2>

        <h3>Phase 1 Use-case framing</h3>
        <p>We don't start with the tools. We start with operations.</p>
        <p>
          Which processes are the most time-consuming, the most exposed to
          errors, the most dependent on scarce resources? Among these processes,
          which are good candidates for AI augmentation and in what order of
          priority?
        </p>
        <p>
          By the end of this phase: a clear mapping of the retained use cases,
          with a defined success criterion for each.
        </p>

        <h3>Phase 2 Vendor selection and architecture</h3>
        <p>Build, buy, or partner? General-purpose LLM or specialised model? Cloud or on-premise?</p>
        <p>
          These decisions depend on your constraints (compliance, data,
          infrastructure) and your AI maturity level. They should never be left
          to a single vendor whose interest is to sell you their solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          Izybiz conducts this selection independently, with technical and
          operational criteria defined upfront.
        </p>

        <h3>Phase 3 AI workflow design</h3>
        <p>
          An AI workflow isn't a standard workflow with an extra tool. It requires
          redefining task sequences, human validation points, threshold rules
          (when AI decides alone, when it proposes, when it escalates), and
          supervision protocols.
        </p>
        <p>This is the most important work and the one most AI projects skip.</p>

        <h3>Phase 4 Deployment and go-live</h3>
        <p>
          Progressive rollout by scope, adoption and quality metrics tracked,
          adjustments made under real conditions. Not a mass rollout that fails
          for lack of adoption.
        </p>

        <h3>Phase 5 Transfer and independence</h3>
        <p>
          Full documentation of systems, team training, maintenance and evolution
          processes put in place. At this stage, the organisation can run,
          adjust and evolve its AI workflows without Izybiz.
        </p>


        <h2>Frequently asked questions about AI integration in business</h2>

        <h3>"AI is too early for us we're not ready."</h3>
        <p>
          84% of AI failures are due to management, not technology. If you wait
          to be "technologically ready", you'll wait forever. The real question
          is: do you have clear use cases, governance in place, and an
          operational lead capable of holding execution? That's what being ready
          means.
        </p>

        <h3>"Our AI projects have already failed. Why would this time work?"</h3>
        <p>
          Because next time will start with framing, not the tool. And because
          there will be an operational lead holding the integration end to end
          not an IT project with no business sponsor and no human checkpoints.
        </p>

        <h3>"How do you measure the ROI of an AI integration?"</h3>
        <p>
          Every retained use case has a success criterion defined during framing:
          time saved, error rate reduced, volume processed, cost per transaction.
          These metrics are tracked in production. ROI is traceable that's a
          condition of the mission, not a hope.
        </p>

        <h3>"Which AI models do you use?"</h3>
        <p>
          Model selection depends on the use case, compliance constraints and
          existing architecture. Izybiz isn't tied to any vendor selection is
          conducted independently based on your stakes. This can include cloud
          models (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google) or locally deployed models depending
          on your requirements.
        </p>

        <h3>"Does AI integration require transforming our data?"</h3>
        <p>
          Often, yes partially. A data audit is part of the framing phase. But
          "transform all the data before starting" is a false excuse. We start
          with use cases whose data is available and clean, and address the
          others in parallel.
        </p>

        <h3>"Will our teams resist?"</h3>
        <p>
          Resistance comes from fear of the unknown and from feeling excluded.
          The Izybiz method involves business teams from the framing stage
          onward they define the use cases, take part in workflow design, and
          are trained before deployment. It's not done for them, it's done with
          them.
        </p>


        <h2>What the mission leaves behind</h2>
        <table class="legacy-article__table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Deliverable</th><th>What it means in practice</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>AI system in production</td><td>One or more workflows running with real volumes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Technical documentation</td><td>Architecture, configurations, connectors, dependencies</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operational documentation</td><td>Workflows, human checkpoints, threshold rules, escalations</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Trained teams</td><td>Understand what they use and can evolve it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Independence</td><td>No consultant needed to maintain and adjust</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>


        <h2>Ready to get out of the abandoned-POC cycle?</h2>
        <p>
          A 30-minute call is enough to clarify whether your situation calls for
          a structured AI integration and whether Izybiz is the right fit to
          execute it.
        </p>
        <p>No pitch. No demo. A conversation between executives.</p>
        <p class="legacy-article__cta-line">
          <a class="legacy-article__cta" href="/en/contact/" data-cta="bottom-cta">Book a call</a>
        </p>


        <p class="legacy-article__footnote">
          <em>See also: <a href="/en/interim-management/">Interim
          management</a> for situations where AI integration is part of a
          broader operational transformation.</em>
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
